The '''2010 UEFA Champions League final''' was an [[association football]] match played at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium]], home of [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]],<ref name="exec_meet">{{cite news|title=Madrid and Hamburg awarded 2010 finals |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/01cb-0f84d3be8863-01dcdabe82b3-1000--madrid-and-hamburg-awarded-2010-finals/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 March 2008 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100323040748/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/aboutuefa/organisation/executivecommittee/news/newsid%3D676743.html |archive-date=23 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> on 22 May 2010, to determine the winners of the [[2009–10 UEFA Champions League]]. It was the first Champions League final to be played on a weekend, rather than the traditional Wednesday.<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League final switched |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/7120518.stm |work=[[BBC Sport]] |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=30 November 2007 |access-date=24 May 2008 }}</ref> The match was won by [[Inter Milan]], who beat [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] 2–0 to complete the [[Treble (association football)|treble]], a feat never before achieved by any team from either Italy or Germany. The refereeing team came from England and was led by [[Howard Webb]].<ref name="referee"/>

The win gave Inter their third European Cup title, and their first since [[1965 European Cup final|1965]]. That 45 years of await broke the record of the longest gap between two UCL titles, which was held by [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] before, with 32 years ([[1966 European Cup final|1966]]-[[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]]). Moreover, it was Inter's first appearance in the final since [[1972 European Cup final|1972]], and they were the first Italian team to appear in the showpiece match since [[Derby della Madonnina|rivals]] [[AC Milan]] won the competition in [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]]. Meanwhile, Bayern had won the competition as recently as [[2001 UEFA Champions League final|2001]]—their most recent final appearance—although they were the first German side to reach the final since [[Bayer 04 Leverkusen|Bayer Leverkusen]] in [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]]. The 2010 final was the first not to feature an English side since [[FC Porto|Porto]] beat [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] in [[2004 UEFA Champions League final|2004]] with [[José Mourinho]] also coaching the winning side, and due to [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] being knocked out by Bayern in the quarter-finals on away goals. This was also the first final to include two group runners-up, both Bayern and Inter finished second in group A and F, respectively. The Santiago Bernabéu Stadium had hosted three European Cup finals before; in [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1969 European Cup final|1969]] and [[1980 European Cup final|1980]].

As the winners, Inter played against [[2009–10 UEFA Europa League]] winners [[Atlético Madrid]] in the [[2010 UEFA Super Cup]], and also entered the semi-finals of the [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup]]. The match was Inter's last game under the management of Mourinho as he would move to Real Madrid a few days after the match.

==Background==
Prior to the 2010 final, [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] and [[Inter Milan]] had met four times in European competition. In those four matches, Bayern held the edge with two wins to Inter's one; the other match finished as a draw. The first meeting between the two sides took place in the [[1988–89 UEFA Cup#Third round|third round of the 1988–89 UEFA Cup]]; Inter won 2–0 the first match at the [[Olympic Stadium (Munich)|Olympiastadion]] in Munich, but Bayern responded with a 3–1 win at the San Siro two weeks later to go through on the [[away goals rule]]. They were next drawn together in [[2006–07 UEFA Champions League group stage|Group B of the 2006–07 UEFA Champions League]], along with [[FC Spartak Moscow|Spartak Moscow]] and [[Sporting CP]]. The first match between Bayern and Inter was played at the San Siro, where Bayern won 2–0; the return match finished as a 1–1 draw.

Both teams went into the final chasing [[Treble (association football)|the treble]], an achievement never before reached by teams from their respective countries; Inter came closest in 1965, when they won the [[1964–65 Serie A|Serie A]] and the [[1965 European Cup final|European Cup]] but lost 1–0 to [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] in the [[1965 Coppa Italia final|Coppa Italia final]]. Bayern Munich claimed their 22nd [[2009–10 Bundesliga|Bundesliga]] title on the last day of the season with a 3–1 win away to [[Hertha BSC]] on 8 May 2010,<ref>{{cite news |title=Champions Munich finish with a flourish |url=http://www.fcbayern.telekom.de/en/news/matchreport/2010/23368.php?fcb_sid=b183cefd532137b5d2ee1031044b5612 |work=fcbayern.de |publisher=FC Bayern |date=8 May 2010 |access-date=10 May 2010 }}</ref> before claiming their eighth [[Double (association football)|domestic Double]] with a 4–0 win over [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]] in the [[2010 DFB-Pokal Final]] on 15 May.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bayern storm to domestic double triumph |url=http://www.fcbayern.telekom.de/en/news/matchreport/2010/23463.php?fcb_sid=5110e23580454c02c49ae638855fddcc |work=fcbayern.de |publisher=FC Bayern |date=15 May 2010 |access-date=16 May 2010 }}</ref> Meanwhile, Inter beat [[AS Roma|Roma]] 1–0 for their sixth [[2010 Coppa Italia Final|Coppa Italia]] on 5 May,<ref>{{cite news |title=Inter-Roma: 1-0, la Tim Cup ai nerazzurri |trans-title=Inter-Roma: 1-0, the Tim Cup to the Nerazzuri |url=http://www.inter.it/aas/news/reader?L=it&N=48216&stringa= |work=inter.it |publisher=F.C. Internazionale Milano |date=5 May 2010 |access-date=10 May 2010 |archive-date=10 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121010193647/http://www.inter.it/aas/news/reader?L=it&N=48216&stringa= |url-status=dead }}</ref> and then won their fifth Serie A title in a row and their second Double on 16 May with a 1–0 win away to relegated [[S.S. Robur Siena|Siena]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Jose Mourinho's Treble-chasing Inter Milan win Serie A |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8685518.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=16 May 2010 |access-date=16 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100519130930/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8685518.stm |archive-date=19 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> With both teams having secured domestic Doubles going into the final, it was guaranteed that the Treble would be won for the second year in a row, following [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]]'s success in [[FC Barcelona season 2008–09|2008–09]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Jones |first=Grahame L. |title=Champions League final pits top coaches, World Cup stars |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2010-may-21-la-sp-champions-league-20100522-story.html |access-date=28 May 2010 |newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=21 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100528003712/http://articles.latimes.com/2010/may/21/sports/la-sp-champions-league-20100522 |archive-date=28 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The managers of both teams had won the Champions League before: Bayern manager [[Louis van Gaal]] won the competition with [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] in [[1995 UEFA Champions League final|1995]], while Inter's [[José Mourinho]] was manager of [[FC Porto|Porto]]'s [[2004 UEFA Champions League final|2004]] side. The winning manager would therefore become only the third in European Cup history to win as manager of two clubs, following in the footsteps of [[Ernst Happel]] ([[Feyenoord]] in [[1970 European Cup final|1970]] and [[Hamburger SV]] in [[1983 European Cup final|1983]]) and [[Ottmar Hitzfeld]] ([[Borussia Dortmund]] in [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]] and Bayern Munich in [[2001 UEFA Champions League final|2001]]).<ref>{{cite news|first=Michael |last=Harrold |title=Euphoric Inter face final challenge |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e4-0e746f89abb1-1ef3c3f806b1-1000--euphoric-inter-face-final-challenge/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=29 April 2010 |access-date=5 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100505141900/http://en.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1482695.html |archive-date=5 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Estadio Santiago Bérnabeu (Mayo 2010) (4624134213).jpg|thumb|The Santiago Bernabéu Stadium was selected as the venue for the 2010 UEFA Champions League final in March 2008.]]

The [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium]] in [[Madrid]] was selected as the venue for the 2010 UEFA Champions League final at a meeting of the UEFA Executive Committee in [[Vaduz]], [[Liechtenstein]], on 28 March 2008. The committee – who selected the venue for the [[2010 UEFA Europa League Final]] at the same meeting – based their decision on a number of criteria, including stadium capacity, facilities and security.<ref name="exec_meet"/> It had been decided that the final would be played on a Saturday for the first time in Champions League history at the UEFA Executive Committee's meeting in [[Lucerne]], [[Switzerland]], on 30 November 2007.<ref>{{cite news |first=Mark |last=Chaplin |title=Champions League changes agreed |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/01c8-0f84bc7ad9f5-707744eaabf4-1000--champions-league-changes-agreed/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=1 December 2007 |access-date=9 March 2010 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Ashby |first=Kevin |date=22 May 2010 |title=Saturday night UEFA Champions League fever |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e5-0e749b889d87-02f7d4306c85-1000--saturday-night-uefa-champions-league-fever/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100523193106/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1491273.html |archive-date=23 May 2010 |access-date=22 May 2010 |publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2007-11-30 |title=Champions League final switched |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/7120518.stm |access-date=2024-02-24 |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Champions final moved to Saturday - CNN.com |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2007/SPORT/football/11/30/uefa.final/ |access-date=2024-02-24 |website=CNN}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=November 30, 2007 |title=Champions League final to switch to a Saturday |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-europe-champions/champions-league-final-to-switch-to-a-saturday-idUKL3090458220071130/?edition-redirect=uk |access-date=February 24, 2024 |website=[[Reuters]]}}</ref>

[[File:Il Santiago Bernabeu.jpg|thumb|The front side of Bernabéu during the final.]]

The stadium was opened in 1947 following the election of [[Santiago Bernabéu (footballer)|Santiago Bernabéu]] as the president of Real Madrid. Upon construction, the stadium had a maximum capacity in excess of 75,000, but this was increased to 125,000 with the addition of a fourth stand in 1954. The stadium was chosen as one of two venues for matches at the [[1964 European Nations' Cup]], hosting both of the [[Spain national football team|Spain]] team's matches, including their 2–1 win over the [[Soviet Union national football team|Soviet Union]]. In preparation for the [[1982 FIFA World Cup]], FIFA requirements forced renovations to the stadium, adding a canopy to three of the four stands and reducing the capacity to 90,800. The stadium hosted all three Group B matches and the final of the 1982 World Cup. Conversion to an all-seater stadium in 1998 further reduced capacity to just over 75,000, but the most recent expansion in 2006 increased capacity to just over 80,000. Only around 75,000 seats, however, were available for the 2010 final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Santiago Bernabéu |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/final/stadium/index.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 January 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100212050106/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/final/stadium/index.html |archive-date=12 February 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The stadium had hosted three [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League winners|European Cup finals]]: in [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1969 European Cup final|1969]] and [[1980 European Cup final|1980]]. [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] themselves won the 1957 final – their second of five consecutive wins – beating [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] 2–0 in front of 120,000 spectators, the second highest attendance in a European Cup final. [[A.C. Milan]] won the next final at the stadium, beating [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] 4–1 in 1969, and [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]] won 1–0 against [[Hamburger SV]] in the most recent final in Madrid in 1980.<ref>{{cite web |title=Previous finals in the city |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/final/previousfinalsincity/index.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 January 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100212050101/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/final/previousfinalsincity/index.html |archive-date=12 February 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

