==Pre-match==

===Identity===
The unique visual identity of the 2010 final was revealed at a special ceremony at Madrid's ''Ciudad del Fútbol Español'' on 20 November 2009. In attendance at the ceremony were final ambassador [[Emilio Butragueño]], [[Royal Spanish Football Federation]] president Ángel María Villar Llona and UEFA's competitions director Giorgio Marchetti. UEFA has given the Champions League final a unique visual identity every year since 1999, in order to give "a distinctive flavour of the host city".<ref>{{cite news|title=Logo launch to herald Madrid final |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=919829.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=13 November 2009 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100301035402/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D919829.html |archive-date=1 March 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The logo features the UEFA Champions League trophy at its core, surrounded by elements of the Champions League "starball" logo, the Santiago Bernabéu Stadium and the [[Puerta de Alcalá]]. The logo was designed to capture "the cultural and 'fiesta' aspects which Madrid is famed for".<ref>{{cite news|first=Paul |last=Bryan |title=Madrid proud to be in final spotlight |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01df-0e735c41a22e-746ec7dba2d7-1000--madrid-proud-to-be-in-final-spotlight/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 November 2009 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100301035407/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D922221.html |archive-date=1 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
Although the usual capacity of the Santiago Bernabéu Stadium was over 80,000, the net capacity for the 2010 Champions League final was around 75,000. The two finalists were each allocated 21,000 tickets, with a further 11,000 tickets being made available to the general public. Applications for those tickets were opened on 8 March 2010 and ran until 19 March; recipients were determined by a random lottery. These figures included a certain number of tickets set aside specifically for children; 2,000 tickets from each club's allocation went to children and their accompanying adults, as did 1,000 tickets from the general public's allocation. Finally, 500 tickets were reserved for children taking part in the activities at the UEFA Champions Festival in the week leading up to the final.<ref>{{cite news|title=Champions League final tickets on sale |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e3-0e73f5b04686-2ffca841748c-1000--champions-league-final-tickets/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=8 March 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100318021826/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1458258.html |archive-date=18 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

A ticketing launch event was held in Madrid on 5 March 2010, at which the ticketing concept for the final was announced. The event was also used to promote the start of ticket sales for the match, and was attended by the ambassador for the final, [[Emilio Butragueño]], the president of the [[Community of Madrid]], [[Esperanza Aguirre]], and representatives from [[UEFA]], the [[Royal Spanish Football Federation]] and the host club, [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Final countdown continues with ticketing launch |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e2-0e73e71c0524-1e92ed9bdef7-1000--final-countdown-continues-with-ticketing-launch/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=25 February 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100227015041/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1454584.html |archive-date=27 February 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> At the event, Aguirre was presented with the first ticket for the final by Real Madrid players [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] and [[Raúl (footballer)|Raúl]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Ticket launch raises expectation |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e3-0e73f341502c-a69aa67f3b1a-1000--ticket-launch-raises-expectation/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=5 March 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100307065221/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1458364.html |archive-date=7 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Related events===
A trophy handover ceremony was held at the Palacio de Cibeles in Madrid on 16 April 2010, when [[Johan Cruyff]] and [[Joan Laporta]] – as representatives of the 2009 champions, [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] – returned the UEFA Champions League Trophy to UEFA president [[Michel Platini]]. Platini then handed the trophy to Royal Spanish Football Federation president Ángel María Villar Llona and [[Alberto Ruiz-Gallardón]], the mayor of Madrid, so that it might be put on display in the city until the day of the final. Real Madrid president [[Florentino Pérez]], players [[Raúl (footballer)|Raúl]], [[Kaká]] and [[Cristiano Ronaldo]], and final ambassador Emilio Butragueño were also present at the ceremony.<ref>{{cite news|title=Madrid to receive UEFA Champions League Trophy |url=http://www.uefa.com/uefa/mediaservices/newsid=1475272.html |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 April 2010 |access-date=11 April 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100412062434/http://www.uefa.com/uefa/mediaservices/newsid%3D1475272.html |archive-date=12 April 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|first=Paul |last=Bryan |title=Handover makes Madrid proud hosts |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e4-0e7458e90f7c-bab5e9b77401-1000--handover-makes-madrid-proud-hosts/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 April 2010 |access-date=21 April 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100420062955/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1477227.html |archive-date=20 April 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Match ball===
[[File:2010 UEFA Champions League Final ball.JPG|thumb|upright|A ball from the final on display at the 2011 UEFA Champions Festival in Hyde Park, London.]]

The official match ball for the 2010 Champions League final, the ''[[Adidas Finale]] Madrid'', was unveiled on 9 March 2010. It was the tenth ball to use the "Starball" design that had become synonymous with the UEFA Champions League. Each of the stars on the ball featured an element of the logos of each of the last 10 Champions League finals, drawn in gold. In reference to the colours of the [[Flag of Spain|Spanish flag]], each gold star has a red border, while the base colour of the ball referred to the white of [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]'s kit. The ball retained the "goosebump" texture of the previous two versions, but the panel configuration was changed for the ''Finale Madrid'', with the panels following the star pattern on the ball.<ref>{{cite news|title=Finale Madrid starball takes flight |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e3-0e73f8aa766c-b44fbd2a8c0d-1000--finale-madrid-starball-takes-flight/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 March 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100311231155/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1459626.html |archive-date=11 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=adidas Finale X Madrid Football |url=http://www.soccerbible.com/news/general/archive/2010/03/09/adidas-Finale-X-Madrid-Football.aspx |work=SoccerBible.com |publisher=SoccerBible |date=9 March 2010 |access-date=9 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100413080032/http://www.soccerbible.com/news/general/archive/2010/03/09/adidas-Finale-X-Madrid-Football.aspx |archive-date=13 April 2010 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:Howard Webb3.jpg|thumb|upright|left|Match referee Howard Webb had been on the list of FIFA-accredited referees since 2005.]]

The referee for the 2010 UEFA Champions League final was [[Howard Webb]], representing [[The Football Association]] of England.<ref name="referee"/> Webb became an international referee in 2005,<ref>{{cite web|title=England – Men's Referees List |url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/developing/refereeing/association=eng/peoplekind=ref.html |work=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=31 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090620001727/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/developing/refereeing/association%3Deng/peoplekind%3Dref.html |archive-date=20 June 2009 }}</ref> and took charge of his first UEFA Champions League match in the August of that year, when he officiated the match between [[FC Haka|Haka]] and [[Vålerenga Fotball|Vålerenga]] in the second qualifying round. His first appointment in the Champions League proper came a year later with the group stage encounter between [[FCSB|Steaua București]] and [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]] on 26 September 2006. Prior to the 2010 final, he had refereed a further 17 Champions League matches and nine [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] matches. He was also a referee at [[UEFA Euro 2008]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Referee Webb chosen for Euro 2008 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/internationals/7152694.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=19 December 2007 |access-date=1 June 2010 }}</ref> the [[2009 FIFA Confederations Cup]],<ref>{{cite news|title=FIFA appoints match officials |url=https://www.fifa.com/confederationscup/media/newsid=1054745.html |work=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=5 May 2009 |access-date=1 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090509162716/http://www.fifa.com/confederationscup/media/newsid%3D1054745.html |archive-date=9 May 2009 |df=dmy }}</ref> and he was selected to be England's representative at the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]].<ref>{{cite news|title=30 referees from 28 countries appointed for 2010 FIFA World Cup |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/organisation/media/newsid=1166692.html |work=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=5 February 2010 |access-date=9 April 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100417080223/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/organisation/media/newsid%3D1166692.html |archive-date=17 April 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

As usual, the referee was supported by assistant referees and a fourth official from the same country; in the 2010 final, Howard Webb was assisted by Darren Cann and Mike Mullarkey, with [[Martin Atkinson]] as the fourth official.<ref name="referee"/>

===Team selection===
The Inter team that began the game featured no Italian players in the starting line-up, while Bayern started the game with five Germans, all internationals named in the provisional [[2010 FIFA World Cup squads#Germany|German squad for the 2010 World Cup]]. [[Franck Ribéry]] was a notable absence for Bayern, serving a three-match suspension after being sent off in the first leg of the semi-final against [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]] for a foul on [[Lisandro López (footballer, born 1983)|Lisandro López]], while Inter were without midfielder [[Thiago Motta]], who was serving a two-match suspension.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e5-0e7479154c80-12fa0abaabd2-1000--motta-suspended-for-two-matches/?iv=true&referrer=%2Finsideuefa%2Fdisciplinary%2Fnews%2Fnewsid%3D1485864|title=Motta suspended for two matches|publisher=UEFA|date=7 May 2010|access-date=7 April 2020}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
[[File:2010 Champions League Final opening ceremony.jpg|thumb|More than 100 people were involved in the opening ceremony.]]

The 2010 Champions League final officially opened with the UEFA Champions Festival on 15 May 2010. The festival was held at Madrid's ''[[Buen Retiro Park|Parque del Retiro]]'' and ran for the entire week leading up to the final. It featured several events and exhibitions for fans to take part in; as well as being able to play on public-use mini-pitches, fans were able to meet famous faces from European football and explore the history of the Champions League. On the day of the final, the festival culminated with a match between former Spanish players and other European ex-professionals.<ref>{{cite news|title=Champions Festival |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e4-0e7439a4b735-7a62971c208e-1000--champions-festival/ |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 April 2010 |access-date=29 April 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100501141314/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1467122.html |archive-date=1 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The [[UEFA Champions League Anthem]] was performed by Peruvian tenor [[Juan Diego Flórez]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://operawire.com/jonas-kaufmann-the-other-opera-stars-who-have-featured-at-the-champions-league-final/|title = Jonas Kaufmann & the Other Opera Stars Who Have Featured at the Champions League Final|date = 2 June 2017}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Forza Inter!.jpg|thumb|The Inter Milan fans unfurled a huge banner prior to kick-off.]]

The match was won 2–0 by Inter. Inter employed a counter-attacking strategy that saw them have less possession than Bayern, but Inter were able to comfortably defend their lead.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.smh.com.au/sport/football/maestro-mourinho-scores-perfect-inter-farewell-in-champions-league-final-20100523-w3bc.html |title=Maestro Mourinho scores perfect Inter farewell in Champions League final |last=Hassett |first=Sebastian |date=23 May 2010 |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]]|access-date=22 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100524173133/http://www.smh.com.au/sport/football/maestro-mourinho-scores-perfect-inter-farewell-in-champions-league-final-20100523-w3bc.html |archive-date=24 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> Both goals were scored by [[Diego Milito]], in the 35th and 70th minutes. Milito's first was scored following a long clearance by Inter goalkeeper [[Júlio César (football goalkeeper, born 1979)|Júlio César]] flicked down by Milito to [[Wesley Sneijder]], who returned the pass to Milito to score. Milito's second goal came after he collected a pass from [[Samuel Eto'o]] and beat [[Daniel Van Buyten]] with the ball.<ref>{{cite news |first=Sam |last=Lyon |title=Bayern Munich 0-2 Inter Milan |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8697017.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=22 May 2010 |access-date=28 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160112103818/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8697017.stm |archive-date=12 January 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref> Milito was substituted shortly before the end of the match, allowing him to be applauded by the Inter supporters.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.foxsports.com.au/story/0,8659,27164577-23215,00.html |title=Inter Milan defeat Bayern Munich in Champions League final in Madrid |date=23 May 2010 |work=Agence France-Presse |publisher=Fox Sports |access-date=22 May 2010 }}</ref> After the match, Milito stated the win brought "incredible happiness" and claimed his side deserved its victory.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,11860_6168179,00.html |title=Milito 'absolutely happy' |last=Carminati |first=Nadia |date=22 May 2010 |work=Sky Sports |access-date=22 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100524170143/http://www.skysports.com/story/0%2C19528%2C11860_6168179%2C00.html |archive-date=24 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> Bayern captain [[Mark van Bommel]] conceded Inter was the "most effective team", referring to the success of Inter's counter-attacking tactics.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/1716/champions-league/2010/05/22/1937789/the-most-effective-team-has-won-bayern-munich-skipper-mark |title=The Most Effective Team Has Won – Bayern Munich Skipper Mark Van Bommel |last=Coerts |first=Stefan |date=22 May 2010 |work=goal.com |access-date=22 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100525084539/http://www.goal.com/en/news/1716/champions-league/2010/05/22/1937789/the-most-effective-team-has-won-bayern-munich-skipper-mark |archive-date=25 May 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> José Mourinho revealed after the match that he would likely resign from Inter to pursue the goal of being the first manager to win the Champions League with three clubs. Reports had linked him with a move to Real Madrid.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/sports/soccer/2010-05-22-862990020_x.htm |title=Mourinho says he'll probably leave Inter |date=22 May 2010 |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=22 May 2010 }}</ref>

