==Post-match==
[[File:San Siro Museum, Milan (Ank Kumar, Infosys Limited) 16.jpg|thumb|Match winner [[Diego Milito]]'s jersey and memorabilia from the final displayed at the [[San Siro]] museum]]
As a result of Inter's victory, Italy held onto its position in the top three of the [[UEFA coefficient#Men's Association coefficient|UEFA country coefficient rankings]] and would therefore retain its fourth berth in the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League]]. Had Bayern won or the match had gone to penalties, Germany would have overtaken Italy and received an extra entry in the following season.<ref>{{cite news |title=National Champions League status at stake for Inter and Bayern Munich |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/may/02/champions-league-status-inter-bayern |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=2 May 2010 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref> Having beaten [[AS Roma|Roma]] in both the [[2010 Coppa Italia final]] and to the [[2009–10 Serie A]] title, Inter became the first ever Italian club to have won the [[Treble (association football)|treble]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Amy |last=Lawrence |title=Trebles all round to celebrate rarity becoming routine |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2010/may/22/bayern-munich-inter-treble-bid |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=22 May 2010 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref> As of the [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League|2024–25 season]], Inter would appear in two more Champions League finals, losing both to treble-winning sides in [[2023 UEFA Champions League final|2023]] and [[2025 UEFA Champions League final|2025]]. They are still the most recent Italian club to have won the Champions League, and the last to have won a European trophy until Roma won the [[2022 UEFA Europa Conference League final|inaugural UEFA Europa Conference League final in 2022]].

Despite winning the treble, Mourinho resigned as manager of Inter on 28 May to take over from [[Manuel Pellegrini]] as manager of [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], citing a desire to become the first manager to win the Champions League with three clubs.<ref>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Hayward |title=José Mourinho's treble – now for the Real story |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2010/may/23/inter-champions-league-mourinho-real |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=23 May 2010 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref> However, he was unable to accomplish this feat, being eliminated in three successive semi-finals before returning to his former club [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in June 2013,<ref>{{cite news |title=Jose Mourinho returns as Chelsea manager on four-year deal |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/22574810 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=3 June 2013 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref> with [[Carlo Ancelotti]] replacing him at Real Madrid; he would gone on to win the elusive Champions League title in his [[2013–14 Real Madrid CF season|first season there]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Carlo Ancelotti named Real Madrid boss, Laurent Blanc joins PSG |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/23047006 |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation|date=25 June 2013 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref>

Bayern would end their wait for the Champions League title in [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]] and securing their own treble, but not after losing another final, held at their [[Allianz Arena|home stadium]] in [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|the previous year]], to Chelsea. As of 2025, they would appear in one more Champions League final post-2013, when they won in [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020]] to secure a second treble.

As winners of the Champions League, Inter played in the [[2010 UEFA Super Cup]] against [[2009–10 UEFA Europa League]] winners [[Atlético Madrid]]. They lost the match 2–0, with goals from [[José Antonio Reyes]] and [[Sergio Agüero]]. They also took part in the [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup]], entering at the semi-finals. There they beat [[2010 AFC Champions League]] winners [[Seongnam FC|Seongnam Ilhwa Chunma]] 3–0<ref>{{cite news |title=Inter Milan secure Club World Cup final place |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/9293627.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation|date=16 December 2010 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref> to reach the final against [[2010 CAF Champions League]] winners [[TP Mazembe]], whom they also beat 3–0 to take the title.<ref>{{cite news |title=Inter Milan beat TP Mazembe to take World Club crown |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/9301760.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation|date=18 December 2010 |access-date=7 July 2015 }}</ref>

